From 9bb6d8e24e198b4bbad249feac8a52120c8c8fc8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 10:59:17 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] whm: Add Channel Scan support with BSS Load utilization
 scaling support

The pWHM Channel Scan path (mon_wlan_hal_whm) supports both a fresh scan
(WiFi.Radio.*.startScan()) and fetching already-available results
(WiFi.Radio.*.getScanResults() / getScanCombinedData()), but three defects
kept it from producing spec-compliant Channel Scan Reports and made the
fresh-scan flow unreliable, especially on 5GHz DFS channels.

1. DFS Background CAC causes startScan() to fail (Resource busy)
   A fresh Channel Scan on PRPLOS/pWHM frequently failed to ever start.
   The wld driver returned NL80211_CMD_TRIGGER_SCAN -16 (Resource busy):

     wld: nlCore  - nl error:-16 (Resource busy) (cmd:33:NL80211_CMD_TRIGGER_SCAN)
     wld: wldScan - wlan1: scan start failed -5 / error in starting scan
     mon_wlan_hal_whm.cpp --> remote function call startScan Failed!
     monitor.cpp --> channel_scan_trigger Failed

   Root cause (confirmed from field logs on the 5GHz radio): the driver
   refuses the foreground scan with "wlan1: rad not ready for scan
   (state:BG_CAC)" because the radio is performing a mandatory DFS
   Background CAC (Channel Availability Check / radar detection) on the
   requested 5GHz DFS channels (52/56/60/64). 2.4GHz (non-DFS) has no CAC
   and succeeds on the first try.

   The generic radio "Status" DM parameter is USELESS for detecting this -
   it reports 'Up' throughout CAC. The correct, driver-authoritative signal
   is "WiFi.Radio.{i}.ChannelMgt.RadioStatus", confirmed by field
   inspection to read "BG_CAC_NS" while CAC is running and transition to
   "Up" the instant CAC completes.

   Fix channel_scan_trigger():
   - Always issue stopScan() before starting, unconditionally, so the wld
     scan resource is released regardless of which entity holds it (not
     just scans this HAL started).
   - Poll ChannelMgt.RadioStatus (not "Status") and defer startScan()
     while CAC is in progress, waiting up to 650s to cover the full
     regulatory CAC duration (60s typ., up to 600s on some ETSI
     weather-radar channels). Fail open (proceed to startScan()) if
     RadioStatus cannot be read.
   - Once RadioStatus indicates ready, retry startScan() up to 3 times
     with a 300ms settle delay for genuinely transient busy conditions,
     re-checking RadioStatus between attempts in case CAC restarted
     (e.g. a radar hit).
   - On exhaustion (CAC timeout or transient-busy retries exhausted),
     push Channel_Scan_Aborted so ChannelScanTask retries at its own
     cadence instead of hanging in WAIT_FOR_RESULTS_READY.

   Added [CAC] and channel_scan_trigger debug logs (pre-scan stop,
   CAC wait/defer, per-attempt start success/failure, exhaustion) for
   field diagnosis.

2. Fresh-scan race / hang prevention
   channel_scan_trigger() set m_scan_active=true only AFTER calling
   startScan() and pushing Channel_Scan_Triggered. This left two windows
   that stranded the ChannelScanTask FSM in WAIT_FOR_RESULTS_READY until
   it timed out and aborted:
     a) A fast ScanComplete=done arriving the instant startScan() returned
        was dropped by process_scan_complete_event() as "not requested by
        bwl" because m_scan_active was still false.
     b) A startScan() failure returned false without emitting
        Channel_Scan_Aborted, so the task waited for a completion that
        never came.
   Fix: arm m_scan_active BEFORE startScan(); on startScan() failure roll
   back the flag and push Channel_Scan_Aborted so the task retries; on a
   Channel_Scan_Triggered enqueue failure, abort the scan so the radio is
   not left scanning while the task is unaware.

   Also raised PassiveChannelTime to a 150ms floor (independent of the
   caller-supplied dwell_time_msec). Channels that have not completed DFS
   CAC are forced into passive-only scanning by regulation - no probe
   request can be sent, so discovery depends purely on the target AP's
   own beacon happening to fire during the dwell window. Confirmed in the
   field: a 20ms dwell (fine for 2.4GHz active scanning) returned a
   completely empty result on 5GHz DFS channels 52/56/60/64.
   ActiveChannelTime is left unchanged.

3. BSS Load Channel Utilization scaling
   pWHM reports the BSS Load "ChannelUtilization" as the raw 802.11 byte
   (0..255) - e.g. 133 - but the EasyMesh Channel Scan Result TLV /
   sChannelScanResults expects a 0..100 percentage. The value was being
   copied verbatim, so the Controller saw out-of-range utilization
   (e.g. "133%"). Scale it as (raw * 100) / 255, matching the nl80211
   backend. StationCount now also sets load_bss_ie_present so the
   neighbor is reported with "Utilization Present: True".

   NOTE: the BSS Load fields (ChannelUtilization / StationCount) are only
   populated by pWHM when QBSS reporting is enabled on the APs:
       ubus-cli WiFi.AccessPoint.*.Qbss.Enable=1

   Both the fresh-scan and cached-result paths share the same result
   formatter, so the utilization scaling fix applies to both.

   Added [CHAN-SCAN] debug logs (per-BSS channel_utilization with raw
   value, station_count) to make field debugging of the BSS Load /
   utilization values straightforward.

Signed-off-by: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
---
 common/beerocks/bwl/whm/mon_wlan_hal_whm.cpp | 217 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 195 insertions(+), 22 deletions(-)

diff --git a/common/beerocks/bwl/whm/mon_wlan_hal_whm.cpp b/common/beerocks/bwl/whm/mon_wlan_hal_whm.cpp
index 8cd15bc..a01d681 100644
--- a/common/beerocks/bwl/whm/mon_wlan_hal_whm.cpp
+++ b/common/beerocks/bwl/whm/mon_wlan_hal_whm.cpp
@@ -16,8 +16,11 @@
 
 #include <easylogging++.h>
 
+#include <algorithm>
+#include <chrono>
 #include <cmath>
 #include <iomanip>
+#include <thread>
 #include <unordered_set>
 #include <unistd.h>
 #include <utility>
@@ -685,40 +688,199 @@ bool mon_wlan_hal_whm::channel_scan_trigger(int dwell_time_msec,
         channels.pop_back();
     }
 
-    if (m_scan_active) {
-        AmbiorixVariant result_abort;
-        AmbiorixVariant args_abort(AMXC_VAR_ID_HTABLE);
-        //scan is already active, as per spec, cancel the old one and start new one
-        if (!m_ambiorix_cl.call(m_radio_path, "stopScan", args_abort, result_abort)) {
-            LOG(INFO) << " remote function stopScan startScan Failed!";
-        }
-        m_scan_active = false; // optimistically reset m_scan_active
-        LOG(INFO) << "m_scan_active: " << m_scan_active;
-    }
-
     /**
      *Since it is mentioned that the custom dwell time parameter can be set with the prpl
      *controller in the future, it is more appropriate to set the dwell time from here
      */
+    // PassiveChannelTime must be long enough to reliably catch a beacon (typical Beacon
+    // Interval ~100-102.4ms), NOT just the caller's dwell_time_msec. Channels that have not
+    // completed DFS CAC (e.g. 52-64 when the radio itself is parked elsewhere, such as on
+    // 36/80MHz) are FORCED into passive-only scanning by regulation - no probe request can be
+    // sent, so discovery depends purely on the target AP's own beacon happening to fire during
+    // our dwell window. Confirmed in the field: with PassiveChannelTime==ActiveChannelTime==20ms
+    // (the same short dwell that works fine for 2.4GHz ACTIVE scanning - probe req -> near
+    // instant probe resp), the very first 5GHz off-channel scan of 52/56/60/64 returned
+    // completely empty ("ssid_results_as_list are empty"), and subsequent scans only sometimes
+    // caught a lone BSS by chance (Wireshark: "Scan Type: Passive scan" on those channels).
+    // ActiveChannelTime keeps using the caller-supplied dwell_time_msec unchanged - this floor
+    // ONLY raises PassiveChannelTime, so active-scan timing (e.g. 2.4GHz, or 5GHz channels that
+    // HAVE completed CAC and can be actively probed) is unaffected.
+    static constexpr int PASSIVE_CHANNEL_TIME_FLOOR_MS = 150;
+    const int passive_channel_time = std::max(dwell_time_msec, PASSIVE_CHANNEL_TIME_FLOOR_MS);
+    if (passive_channel_time != dwell_time_msec) {
+        LOG(DEBUG) << "channel_scan_trigger: flooring PassiveChannelTime to "
+                   << passive_channel_time << "ms (requested dwell_time_msec=" << dwell_time_msec
+                   << "ms) to reliably catch a beacon during forced-passive DFS scanning on "
+                   << m_radio_path;
+    }
+
     AmbiorixVariant new_map(AMXC_VAR_ID_HTABLE);
-    new_map.add_child("PassiveChannelTime", dwell_time_msec);
+    new_map.add_child("PassiveChannelTime", passive_channel_time);
     new_map.add_child("ActiveChannelTime", dwell_time_msec);
     bool ret = m_ambiorix_cl.update_object(m_radio_path + "ScanConfig.", new_map);
     if (!ret) {
         LOG(WARNING) << "failed to update Active/PassiveChannelTime for " << m_radio_path;
     }
 
-    AmbiorixVariant result;
-    AmbiorixVariant args(AMXC_VAR_ID_HTABLE);
-    if (!channels.empty()) {
-        args.add_child("channels", channels);
+    // Issue a stopScan() first to clear any lingering pWHM foreground scan. A stopScan() failure
+    // here is non-fatal ("no scan running").
+    {
+        AmbiorixVariant result_abort;
+        AmbiorixVariant args_abort(AMXC_VAR_ID_HTABLE);
+        if (!m_ambiorix_cl.call(m_radio_path, "stopScan", args_abort, result_abort)) {
+            LOG(DEBUG) << "channel_scan_trigger: pre-scan stopScan() on " << m_radio_path
+                       << " returned failure (likely nothing was scanning) - continuing";
+        } else {
+            LOG(DEBUG) << "channel_scan_trigger: pre-scan stopScan() OK on " << m_radio_path;
+        }
+        m_scan_active = false;
+    }
+
+    // Root cause (confirmed from field logs on the 5GHz radio): the driver refuses the foreground
+    // scan with "wlan1: rad not ready for scan (state:BG_CAC)" because the radio is performing a
+    // mandatory DFS Background CAC (Channel Availability Check / radar detection) on the requested
+    // 5GHz DFS channels (52/56/60/64). 2.4GHz (non-DFS) has no CAC and succeeds on the first try.
+    //
+    // The generic radio "Status" DM parameter is USELESS for detecting this - it reports 'Up'
+    // throughout CAC. The correct, driver-authoritative signal is the dedicated
+    // "WiFi.Radio.{i}.ChannelMgt.RadioStatus" sub-object parameter, confirmed by direct field
+    // inspection to read "BG_CAC_NS" while CAC is running and transition to "Up" the instant CAC
+    // completes. Poll THIS parameter (not "Status") before/around each startScan() attempt so we
+    // wait exactly as long as CAC actually takes - no fixed guess, no wasted retries once the
+    // radio is genuinely ready, and no premature giving-up if CAC legitimately needs the full
+    // regulatory duration (60s typ., up to 600s on some ETSI weather-radar channels).
+    //
+    // Poll cadence: check RadioStatus every POLL_INTERVAL; a mismatch (still "*_CAC*"/"*_NS")
+    // means "don't even bother calling startScan() yet" - skip straight to the next sleep instead
+    // of generating a doomed startScan() attempt (and its log spam) on every poll tick. Only call
+    // startScan() once RadioStatus has cleared to a ready state, or if we've never managed to read
+    // RadioStatus at all (fail open - rely on the startScan() result itself in that case).
+    static constexpr auto CAC_MAX_WAIT       = std::chrono::seconds(650); // covers 600s ETSI CAC + margin
+    static constexpr auto CAC_POLL_INTERVAL  = std::chrono::seconds(2);
+    static constexpr int  SCAN_START_MAX_ATTEMPTS = 3; // short retry AFTER RadioStatus looks ready
+
+    auto is_radio_status_busy_with_cac = [](const std::string &radio_status) {
+        // Match case-insensitively on "CAC" so both the confirmed "BG_CAC_NS" and any other
+        // vendor CAC-flavoured value (e.g. plain "CAC", "CAC_NS", "FG_CAC") are covered.
+        std::string status_uc = radio_status;
+        std::transform(status_uc.begin(), status_uc.end(), status_uc.begin(), ::toupper);
+        return status_uc.find("CAC") != std::string::npos;
+    };
+
+    const auto wait_deadline = std::chrono::steady_clock::now() + CAC_MAX_WAIT;
+    bool radio_status_readable = false;
+    for (;;) {
+        std::string radio_status;
+        radio_status_readable =
+            m_ambiorix_cl.get_param(radio_status, m_radio_path + "ChannelMgt.", "RadioStatus") &&
+            !radio_status.empty();
+
+        if (!radio_status_readable) {
+            LOG(DEBUG) << "channel_scan_trigger: could not read ChannelMgt.RadioStatus for "
+                       << m_radio_path << " - proceeding without CAC gating (will rely on "
+                          "startScan() result)";
+            break; // fail open: no CAC visibility, fall through to the startScan attempt below
+        }
+
+        if (!is_radio_status_busy_with_cac(radio_status)) {
+            LOG(DEBUG) << "channel_scan_trigger: radio " << m_radio_path
+                       << " ChannelMgt.RadioStatus='" << radio_status << "' (not CAC) - proceeding";
+            break;
+        }
+
+        LOG(INFO) << "[CAC] channel_scan_trigger: radio " << m_radio_path
+                  << " ChannelMgt.RadioStatus='" << radio_status
+                  << "' - CAC in progress, deferring scan trigger";
+
+        if (std::chrono::steady_clock::now() + CAC_POLL_INTERVAL >= wait_deadline) {
+            LOG(ERROR) << "channel_scan_trigger: radio " << m_radio_path
+                       << " still in CAC (ChannelMgt.RadioStatus='" << radio_status
+                       << "') after " << std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::seconds>(
+                                             CAC_MAX_WAIT).count()
+                       << "s wait - surfacing abort so the task reports the channel(s) as "
+                          "unavailable";
+            m_scan_active = false;
+            event_queue_push(Event::Channel_Scan_Aborted);
+            return false;
+        }
+        std::this_thread::sleep_for(CAC_POLL_INTERVAL);
     }
-    if (!m_ambiorix_cl.call(m_radio_path, "startScan", args, result)) {
-        LOG(ERROR) << " remote function call startScan Failed!";
+
+    // RadioStatus indicates ready (or was unreadable - fail open). Try startScan() with a SHORT
+    // bounded retry only for a genuinely transient busy (e.g. a just-finished scan/ACS pass still
+    // settling) - CAC itself has already been waited out above, so this is no longer a blind
+    // long-running retry.
+    bool started = false;
+    for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= SCAN_START_MAX_ATTEMPTS; ++attempt) {
+        AmbiorixVariant result;
+        AmbiorixVariant args(AMXC_VAR_ID_HTABLE);
+        if (!channels.empty()) {
+            args.add_child("channels", channels);
+        }
+
+        // Set m_scan_active BEFORE issuing startScan(), not after. The pWHM "ScanComplete=done"
+        // notification for a very fast scan can arrive on the event socket the instant startScan()
+        // returns - if m_scan_active were still false at that point, process_scan_complete_event()
+        // would take the "results from a scan not requested by bwl" branch, drop the completion,
+        // and the task FSM would sit in WAIT_FOR_RESULTS_READY until it times out and aborts.
+        // Arming the flag first closes that race; cleared again below if the call fails.
+        m_scan_active = true;
+        if (m_ambiorix_cl.call(m_radio_path, "startScan", args, result)) {
+            started = true;
+            LOG(INFO) << "channel_scan_trigger: startScan OK on " << m_radio_path << " (attempt "
+                      << attempt << "/" << SCAN_START_MAX_ATTEMPTS << ")";
+            break;
+        }
+
+        m_scan_active = false;
+
+        // If RadioStatus is readable, re-check it - a radar hit / new CAC could have started
+        // between our wait-loop above and this startScan() call. If so, bail out of the short
+        // retry immediately and go back to actually waiting for CAC rather than spinning.
+        if (radio_status_readable) {
+            std::string radio_status;
+            if (m_ambiorix_cl.get_param(radio_status, m_radio_path + "ChannelMgt.", "RadioStatus") &&
+                is_radio_status_busy_with_cac(radio_status)) {
+                LOG(WARNING) << "channel_scan_trigger: radio " << m_radio_path
+                             << " entered CAC (ChannelMgt.RadioStatus='" << radio_status
+                             << "') during retry - aborting scan (cannot run during CAC)";
+                event_queue_push(Event::Channel_Scan_Aborted);
+                return false;
+            }
+        }
+
+        LOG(WARNING) << "channel_scan_trigger: startScan failed on " << m_radio_path
+                     << " (attempt " << attempt << "/" << SCAN_START_MAX_ATTEMPTS
+                     << ", transient busy - RadioStatus not CAC)";
+
+        if (attempt < SCAN_START_MAX_ATTEMPTS) {
+            AmbiorixVariant result_abort;
+            AmbiorixVariant args_abort(AMXC_VAR_ID_HTABLE);
+            if (!m_ambiorix_cl.call(m_radio_path, "stopScan", args_abort, result_abort)) {
+                LOG(DEBUG) << "channel_scan_trigger: inter-retry stopScan() on " << m_radio_path
+                           << " returned failure - continuing";
+            }
+            std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(300));
+        }
+    }
+
+    if (!started) {
+        LOG(ERROR) << "channel_scan_trigger: startScan failed on " << m_radio_path << " after "
+                   << SCAN_START_MAX_ATTEMPTS
+                   << " attempt(s) (transient busy, RadioStatus not CAC) - surfacing abort so "
+                      "the task retries";
+        m_scan_active = false;
+        event_queue_push(Event::Channel_Scan_Aborted);
+        return false;
+    }
+
+    if (!event_queue_push(Event::Channel_Scan_Triggered)) {
+        LOG(ERROR) << "Failed to queue Channel_Scan_Triggered for " << m_radio_path;
+        // Abort the scan we just started so we don't leave the radio scanning with the task
+        // unaware (which would again strand the FSM until timeout).
+        channel_scan_abort();
         return false;
     }
-    event_queue_push(Event::Channel_Scan_Triggered);
-    m_scan_active = true;
     LOG(INFO) << "m_scan_active: " << m_scan_active;
     return true;
 }
@@ -772,13 +934,24 @@ bool mon_wlan_hal_whm::channel_scan_dump_cached_results()
         }
 
         if (map.find("ChannelUtilization") != map.end()) {
-            results.channel_utilization = std::stoul(map["ChannelUtilization"]);
-            results.load_bss_ie_present = 1;
+            // pWHM (via getScanCombinedData / QBSS BSS Load) reports Channel Utilization as the
+            // raw 802.11 BSS Load byte (0..255), NOT a percentage. The EasyMesh Channel Scan
+            // Result TLV / sChannelScanResults expects a 0..100 percentage, so scale it here.
+            // This mirrors the nl80211 backend fix ((data[2] * 100) / 255). Without the scale, a
+            // raw value like 133 is reported verbatim as "133%" to the Controller.
+            const uint32_t raw_utilization = std::stoul(map["ChannelUtilization"]);
+            results.channel_utilization    = (raw_utilization * 100) / 255;
+            results.load_bss_ie_present     = 1;
+            LOG(DEBUG) << "[CHAN-SCAN] BSS Load for " << results.bssid
+                       << ": channel_utilization=" << int(results.channel_utilization)
+                       << "% (raw=" << raw_utilization << ")";
         }
 
         if (map.find("StationCount") != map.end()) {
             results.station_count       = std::stoul(map["StationCount"]);
             results.load_bss_ie_present = 1;
+            LOG(DEBUG) << "[CHAN-SCAN] BSS Load for " << results.bssid
+                       << ": station_count=" << results.station_count;
         }
 
         LOG(DEBUG) << "Processing results for BSSID:" << results.bssid
-- 
2.34.1

